WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.600
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
to this short recording

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.630
of our 8.701 lecture.

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:12.820
With this short
discussion, I want

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.460
to introduce the teaching staff
to this course, the instructor,

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:23.230
which is myself, Markus
Klute, and our TA Justin.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.710
So I am faculty in the
physics department since 2009.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:30.430
I received my diploma, which
is my undergraduate study,

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:35.740
in Germany and also my PhD
from the University in Bonn.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.590
I-- with research on the
OPAL experiment, which

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.040
is an experiment on the large
electron positron collider

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.000
at CERN, on the ATLAS
experiment, which

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.860
is in the Large Hadron
Collider, also at CERN, and also

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.130
on the D0 experiment, which
was one of the experiments

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:55.630
at the Tevatron in
Chicago at Fermilab.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:59.950
So after my PhD, I joined
MIT as a postdoc, and later

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.180
as a research scientist.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.060
And I worked on
the CDF experiment

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.460
at the Tevatron and
the CMS experiment

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.770
at the Large Hadron Collider.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:14.110
In 2007, I accepted a
faculty position in Germany,

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.850
where I spent about a year,
before coming back to MIT.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.250
It's not a surprise--
this is CV--

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.830
that I-- my interest
is in particle physics

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.200
at the energy frontier.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.210
I work on design,
the construction,

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.610
the commissioning of detectors.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.230
We made major contribution
to the hydronic calorimeter

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:37.780
in CMS and also the
data acquisition system.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:43.960
Most recently, I was leading the
software and computing project

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.700
within the CMS experiment.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.530
Most exciting-- the physics.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.640
And in 2012, we were able
to discover the Higgs

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.800
boson with the CMS experiment.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.640
And so ATLAS had a
similar experience.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.290
And since then, we were able to
look more deeply, more closely

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:06.910
into how the Higgs boson--

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:07.430
case.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:10.900
We were able to show
couplings to W and Z bosons,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:15.790
to photons via loops of
top quarks and W bosons.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:19.030
But then we looked
into whether or not

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.790
Higgs bosons couple of
fermions like electrons.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.880
So we were able to show
couplings to taus--

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.090
those are the heaviest
brothers of the electrons--

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.375
and most recently,
couplings of Higgs

00:02:30.375 --> 00:02:34.340
bosons to muons, which are
second-generation particles.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.110
So this exploitation and
exploration of the Higgs boson

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.560
is really at the center
of my research portfolio.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.670
We don't just spend our
time analyzing the data

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.100
from the LHC, but we
also look whether or not

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:52.360
new machines can teach
us important information

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.520
about the Higgs boson.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.820
When I'm not doing research,
I have a little family

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.810
who I like to spend time with.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.300
I used to play soccer quite
a bit, and also tennis.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.750
But when you get older, those
kind of contact interactions

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.580
are not very useful
for you anymore.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:10.850
You get injured quite a bit.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:15.370
So I left it to the running
part of those activities.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.550
And I picked up
running quite a bit.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.410
In a couple of weeks
into the semester,

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:25.510
everyone, my and the first
virtual Boston Marathon--

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:30.160
so some of those videos might
have a little bit of a fighting

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:31.930
face in front of you.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.510
But I hope everything
is going well.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.210
Our teaching TA is Justin.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:39.085
Justin is a graduate
student in my group.

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:40.570
He's in the second year.

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:44.140
He took this very class with
Mike Williams last fall.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.260
So he should be well-prepared
to guide you and answer

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:50.230
your questions.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.830
He received his
undergraduate degree

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.770
from the University of
Michigan at Ann Arbor,

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.010
where he was working
on the g-2 experiment.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.470
We'll probably talk about
those kind of experiments

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.510
later in the class as well.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:08.270
Recently, he has been
taking up running as well.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:11.050
But he spends, also, time
on rowing and hiking.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.240
So we, both of us, look
forward to meeting you

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:17.260
in this first class
on Tuesday and hope

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.540
that we have a good time
together with 8.701.

